    "stem": "Madhav draws transversal $n$ across lines $u$ and $v$. Angle $P$ sits below $u$ and to the left of $n$. Angle $Q$ sits below $v$ and to the left of $n$.\n\nOne or more options may be correct. Which two claims about angles $P$ and $Q$ are correct?",
    "item_type": "mcq",
    "options": {
      "A": "Angles $P$ and $Q$ are corresponding angles, because each sits in the same matching position at its crossing.",
      "B": "Corresponding angles need one angle from the crossing with $u$ and one from the crossing with $v$.",
      "C": "Angles $P$ and $Q$ are corresponding angles even when both are taken from the crossing with $u$ alone.",
      "D": "Angles $P$ and $Q$ are same-side interior angles, so their measures must sum to $180^{\\circ}$."
    },
